---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/postscript-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/postscript-alternative');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives/postscript-alternative');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Should a legacy-tool change make us leave Postscript?",
    "answer": "First compare moving within Postscript with replacing the account. Export legacy reports before January 4, 2027 and check the account notice and partner-trigger rebuild requirements."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which alternative fits a managed SMS requirement?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Recart for managed SMS and list growth, with the actual service scope confirmed in writing. Compare Klaviyo or Omnisend when coordinating email and SMS is the main requirement; no universal ranking is established."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does recognition create new SMS permission?",
    "answer": "Postscript documents recognition of existing subscribers without changing consent or suppression. A replacement must preserve permission separately from identity matching."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does the cart discount cost?",
    "answer": "For a $122 basket at 67% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $40.26. An 11% code reduces gross profit from $81.74 to $68.32, before messaging, shipping and other costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-16 container">
<a href="/alternatives" class="link">← All alternatives</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Postscript alternatives: preserve the SMS account before replacing it</h1>
<p class="mt-6 text-xl">Decide whether the problem is legacy tooling, recognition, operator capacity or the channel mix. Each requires a different replacement brief.</p>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-10">
<h2>Check the current account before starting a platform migration</h2>
<p>Sources checked November 25, 2026. The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/postscript-sms-marketing">Postscript listing</a> now describes SMS, RCS and email. Do not base a switch on an old claim that it is SMS-only. Ask which channels and features your account can actually use, and price the proposed configuration.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/15784272-important-changes-to-legacy-popups-campaigns-and-automations">legacy changes notice</a> sets January 4, 2027 as the end of read-only access. Native automations have a one-click migration path; partner-trigger automations require rebuilding. Check your account-specific freeze notice and save reports before removal. Moving to Flow Builder may solve a tooling problem without replacing subscriber infrastructure.</p>
<h2>Write a positive replacement brief</h2>
<p><a href="https://recart.com/fully-managed-sms">Recart’s managed offer</a> covers onboarding, popup work and SMS operation. Evaluate it when your shortage is people to operate the channel. Ask for named responsibilities, approval deadlines and a demonstration with your cart; a service proposal is not proof of better recovery. Keep your email owner involved.</p>
<p><a href="https://apps.shopify.com/klaviyo-email-marketing">Klaviyo</a> lists email, SMS and WhatsApp. It is a candidate when the goal is a shared customer view and coordinated channel work. Require the demo to show your actual Shopify event and exclusions rather than assuming consolidation imports every historical rule.</p>
<p><a href="https://apps.shopify.com/omnisend">Omnisend</a> lists email, SMS, signup forms and abandoned-cart automation. Evaluate a combined capture-and-journey workflow when that reduces the number of handoffs your team maintains. Check destination availability, sending allowance and operator effort in the quote. These are different briefs, not a ranked list; adding an email tool alone need not replace the existing SMS program.</p>
<h2>Recognition is not a transferable permission record</h2>
<p>Postscript’s <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/18352295-guide-to-identity-resolution">Identity Resolution guide</a> describes recognition of existing subscribers, without creating contacts or changing permission. For a returning visitor who neither logs in nor clicks a message, compare which system identifies the cart and why. The replacement must show the matched store/customer record and eligibility decision. Do not mark an unidentified cart as SMS eligible just to match old send volume.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13563863-view-subscribers-included-in-a-segment">subscriber guide</a> documents segment CSV exports and profile details including opt-in source and date. Inspect a real export for the fields you need; a visible profile field is not proof it exists in the CSV. Preserve unsubscribes, purpose, source and dates in a separate reconciliation record where needed. Keep email permission separate from promotional and transactional SMS permission.</p>
<h2>Rehearse failures that matter to this account</h2>
<h3>A reply lands after the sender changes</h3>
<p>A shopper asks whether a gift can arrive in time while the cart text is queued. Assign the old inbox and the replacement inbox an owner through cutover. Use a test handset to send a question, STOP and a later reply; retain receipt, routing and suppression logs. A contact import does not move a conversation or guarantee reply routing. Confirm number release, registration and receiving behavior with both providers before setting the change date.</p>
<h3>A recognized visitor becomes an ambiguous identity</h3>
<p>Use two customers sharing an email address but different phones, plus one customer returning on a new browser. Require the correct Shopify customer and checkout linkage, or quarantine the ambiguous record. Evidence is the enrollment decision and restored basket for each profile, not the number of imported rows. Keep the old recognition identifier as migration history rather than treating it as the new provider’s identity key.</p>
<h3>The customer buys while two queues exist</h3>
<p>Queue a recovery message, place the order, then deliver the original cart event twice and out of order. Both systems must show who cancelled or suppressed the reminder and which event was already processed. Postscript’s <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564032-migrate-legacy-automations-into-automation-flows">legacy migration guide</a> says queued subscribers can finish the legacy sequence. Inspect actual queues even after a flow migration; disabling new enrollment alone is not evidence of an empty queue.</p>
<h3>A checkout collector survives the switch</h3>
<p>Inventory desktop/mobile popups, keywords, landing pages and checkout capture. The <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564332-deactivate-your-postscript-account">deactivation guide</a> lists these surfaces and calls for uninstalling Postscript when another provider takes checkout SMS collection. Coordinate that step with number and data continuity first. Submit one signup through each retained surface and verify its destination, disclosure, permission record and single welcome enrollment.</p>
<h2>Keep the useful cart decisions, remove automatic incentives</h2>
<p>Use these as proposed acceptance exercises, not observed supplier failures. For the retained $122 basket at 67% margin, goods cost stays $40.26. An 11% code removes $13.42 from revenue: $108.58 less $40.26 leaves $68.32 gross profit instead of $81.74. Messaging, shipping, payment fees and returns still need deduction. A migration is not a reason to grant the code to every imported cart.</p>
<p>For shipping shock, restore the correct address and show the current shipping total before suggesting an offer. For gift sizing or an unavailable variant, verify the size guide and availability at click time; do not restore an impossible line item. For stacked coupons, exercise the signup offer plus recovery code in Shopify and inspect the actual total. For a BNPL interruption, return to the valid payment step without promising approval. For a minimum-order block, show which quantity must change. These checks require different evidence: checkout totals, inventory, combination rules, payment destination and quantity validation.</p>
<h2>Release and measure the replacement</h2>
<p>Assign one owner per channel and recovery purpose, and one active sender for each queued action. Recheck purchase and withdrawal state immediately before dispatch, use store-scoped stable event IDs for replay deduplication, and keep a rollback record of cancelled work. Restore only currently eligible events if rolling back. Do not reactivate both systems to recover missed volume.</p>
<p>Compare total message and carrier costs, required service fees and internal operating time on the same destination mix. Measure orders and gross profit against a predeclared eligible holdout sized for your traffic; vendor-attributed revenue and extra recognized visits alone do not establish incremental gain. Stay with Postscript if the current tooling and operator arrangement meet the demonstrated requirements at an acceptable cost.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="FAQ: replacing Postscript" />
</section>
</BaseLayout>
